1
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:03,500
It's only a state of mind.

2
00:11:55,000 --> 00:12:01,000
Brazil is a comedy, science fiction film directed by Terry Gilliam.

3
00:17:52,500 --> 00:17:58,500
Low-level bureaucrat Sam Lowry escapes the monotony of his day-to-day life through a
recurring daydream of himself as a virtuous hero saving a beautiful damsel.

4
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:56,000
Investigating a case that led to the wrongful arrest and eventual death of an innocent man instead of wanted terrorist Harry Tuttle, he meets the
woman from his daydream, and in trying to help her gets caught in a web of mistaken identities, mindless bureaucracy and lies.

5
00:29:47,500 --> 00:29:53,500
Sam Lowry, played by Jonathan Pryce.

6
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:51,000
Harry Tuttle, played by Robert De Niro.

7
00:41:42,500 --> 00:41:48,500
Mrs. Ida Lowry, played by Katherine Helmond.

8
00:47:40,000 --> 00:47:46,000
Mr. Kurtzmann, played by Ian Holm.

9
00:53:37,500 --> 00:53:43,500
Spoor, played by Bob Hoskins.

10
00:59:35,000 --> 00:59:41,000
Jack Lint, played by Michael Palin.

11
01:05:32,500 --> 01:05:38,500
Brazil is a 1985 dystopian science fiction black comedy film directed by Terry
Gilliam and written by Gilliam, Charles McKeown, and Tom Stoppard.

12
01:11:30,000 --> 01:11:36,000
A co-production between the United Kingdom and the United States, the film stars Jonathan Pryce, Robert De Niro,
Katherine Helmond, Ian Holm, Bob Hoskins, Michael Palin, Ian Richardson, Peter Vaughan, and Kim Greist.

13
01:17:27,500 --> 01:17:33,500
The film centres on Sam Lowry, a low-ranking bureaucrat trying to find a woman who appears in his dreams while he is working in a mind-numbing job and living in a small flat, set in a dystopian world in
which there is an over-reliance on poorly maintained (and rather whimsical) machines and where people found guilty of crimes are liable for the costs of their interrogation by torture.

14
01:23:25,000 --> 01:23:31,000
Brazil's satire of technocracy, bureaucracy, hyper-surveillance, corporate statism, and state capitalism is reminiscent of
George Orwell's 1949 novel Nineteen Eighty-Four, and it has been called "Kafkaesque" as well as absurdist.

15
01:29:22,500 --> 01:29:28,500
Sarah Street's British National Cinema (1997) described the film as a "fantasy/satire on bureaucratic
society", and John Scalzi's Rough Guide to Sci-Fi Movies (2005) described it as a "dystopian satire".

16
01:35:20,000 --> 01:35:26,000
Jack Mathews, a film critic and the author of The Battle of Brazil (1987), described the film as "satirizing the
bureaucratic, largely dysfunctional industrial world that had been driving Gilliam crazy all his life".

17
01:41:17,500 --> 01:41:23,500
Despite its title, the film is not about the country Brazil nor does it take place there; it is named after the recurrent theme
song, Ary Barroso's "Aquarela do Brasil", known simply as "Brazil" to British audiences, as performed by Geoff Muldaur.

18
01:47:15,000 --> 01:47:21,000
Although a success in Europe, the film was unsuccessful
in its initial North American release.

19
01:53:12,500 --> 01:53:18,500
It has since become a cult film.

20
01:59:10,000 --> 01:59:16,000
In 1999, the British Film Institute voted Brazil the
54th greatest British film of all time.

21
02:05:07,500 --> 02:05:13,500
In 2017, a poll of 150 actors, directors, writers, producers and critics for
Time Out magazine saw it ranked the 24th best British film ever.

22
02:11:05,000 --> 02:11:11,000
The film explores themes of dreams, government,
bureaucracy, police state, great britain.

23
02:17:02,500 --> 02:17:08,500
The tone is lighthearted and dark and thought_provoking.
